<!--
  Calibrated key classes for MUSIC-Hand-v0.15.  RUN 2, 6 September 2026.
  SUPERSEDES key_physics_patch_2026-09-06.xml, which is kept beside this file.
  NOT YET APPLIED to the working copy or the cluster.

  WHY RUN 2 EXISTS
  ----------------
  Run 1 calibrated the key against down-weight alone and reported that 93 gf
  would be needed to hold the key at the bottom of its travel. That figure is
  absurd against a real grand, whose up-weight is about 20 to 25 gf, and the
  objection is what exposed the real defect:

    THE MODEL HAS NO COULOMB FRICTION IN THE KEY JOINT.

  On a real action, down-weight and up-weight differ by twice the friction.
  Technicians treat this as three linked numbers:
      down-weight  = balance weight + friction
      up-weight    = balance weight - friction
      balance      = (down + up) / 2 ,  friction = (down - up) / 2
  With no friction term, a model cannot separate down-weight from up-weight
  at all. Run 1's spring produced, measured by quasi-static probe:

      white key as built:  down  2.8 gf   up  85.8 gf   friction -41.5 gf
      black key as built:  down  4.2 gf   up 165.0 gf   friction -80.4 gf

  Negative friction is not a physical quantity. The as-built key is inverted:
  almost nothing to start it moving, an enormous force to keep it down.

  Run 2 adds joint friction and calibrates all three numbers together.

  A SOLVER SETTING IS REQUIRED, AND IT IS EASY TO MISS
  ----------------------------------------------------
  MuJoCo's frictionloss silently leaks unless the no-slip pass is enabled.
  Tested in isolation at frictionloss 0.020 N m:

      default solver:            0.005 N m of torque already moved the joint
      noslip_iterations="10":    0.015 N m held, 0.021 N m moved

  So <option> must gain noslip_iterations. The model's current <option> line
  has none, which means friction added without it would do nothing and the
  calibration would silently fail. Change required in defaults.xml:

      <option timestep="0.00208333333" impratio="10" gravity="0 0 0"
              noslip_iterations="10"/>

  TARGET AND RESULT
  -----------------
  Target: down-weight 48 gf and up-weight 22 gf, measured at each key's own
  front edge, matching Steinway New York grand mid-register regulation
  (down 48, up 20 or more, so friction near 13).

  Achieved, by bisection in simulation:
      white:  down 48.1 gf   up 24.1 gf   friction 12.0 gf   balance 36.1 gf
      black:  down 48.1 gf   up 24.6 gf   friction 11.7 gf   balance 36.4 gf

  Both key types are calibrated to the same feel AT THEIR OWN FRONT EDGE,
  which is what a technician regulates for. Their geometry differs, so the
  parameter values differ: the black key's playing point is 96 mm from its
  pivot against the white key's 148 mm.

  No measured down-weight or mass for a grand BLACK key could be found in the
  literature. The black key figures here are therefore targeted to match the
  white keys in feel, not fitted to a measurement. Flag that in any write-up.

  WHERE ON THE KEY, WHICH THE MODEL CAN NOW REPRESENT
  ---------------------------------------------------
  Touch weight goes as one over the distance from the pivot, so it depends on
  where the finger lands. Same spring, same friction, moment arm varied:

    white key
      148 mm   down 48.1   up 24.1   balance 36.1   front edge, reference
      130 mm   down 54.8   up 27.5   balance 41.1   ordinary playing
      110 mm   down 64.7   up 32.5   balance 48.6   reaching between blacks
       95 mm   down 74.9   up 37.6   balance 56.3   thumb wedged in on a chord
       80 mm   down 89.0   up 44.6   balance 66.8   as far back as playable

    black key
       96 mm   down 48.1   up 24.6   balance 36.4   front edge
       80 mm   down 57.7   up 29.5   balance 43.6   ordinary playing
       65 mm   down 71.1   up 36.4   balance 53.7   deep in

  Playing 110 mm in rather than 148 raises down-weight by 35 per cent; 80 mm
  in raises it by 85 per cent.

  THIS IS A RESEARCH RESULT, NOT ONLY A CALIBRATION NOTE.
  A smaller hand is pushed further back on the white keys, because reaching
  between the black keys and placing a thumb inside a chord both move the
  contact point toward the pivot. The model logs contact position, so the
  touch weight a given hand actually meets is computable rather than assumed.
  That is a mechanism by which a standard keyboard costs a small hand more,
  and it is separate from span. It has not been proposed in the lab's
  documents and it should be tested.

  Caveat: the standard position along the key at which technicians measure
  down-weight could not be established from the literature. Conventions in
  circulation disagree. Every figure above therefore names its own measuring
  position rather than claiming a standard one.

  UNCHANGED FROM RUN 1, AND STILL VALID
  -------------------------------------
  key mass 120 g white, from Hirschkorn's weighed grand key (Table A.1 to be
    re-verified directly before publication); 62 g black, scaled, unmeasured
  armature restated as the reflected inertia of the whippen, jack, repetition
    lever and hammer, which a single-body key must lump somewhere
  damping 0.30, calibrated against key-speed bands but bracketed rather than
    settled, because the two published sources disagree: Furuya's forces and
    speeds are from a Yamaha U1 upright, Askenfelt and Jansson's speeds from
    a grand. Agrees with both at piano, with the grand alone at forte.

  GRAVITY
  -------
  These figures assume the gravity field stays at zero. Run 1 first said
  gravity would add resistance; that was wrong and was corrected. The key
  body's centre of mass sits on the playing side of its hinge, so gravity
  drives the key DOWN by 60 gf equivalent at the white-key front. With the
  spring below and gravity on, the key falls and stays down.

  Deeper cause: a real grand key pivots at a balance rail 217.2 mm along a
  key spanning about 421 mm, so half its mass counterbalances the front. This
  model hinges the key at one END. Absorbing that in the spring preload works
  but makes the spring do a counterweight's job. The honest fix is a
  counterweight body behind the hinge, which would also bring the key's
  inertia nearer a real one. Not built.

  WHAT IS STILL NOT MODELLED
  --------------------------
  No hammer, no whippen, no jack, no repetition lever. Therefore no
  escapement, no let-off step, no drop, no after-touch, and no hammer whose
  velocity determines the sound.
-->

<default class="white_key">
  <!-- travel depth 0.01 m at 0.148 m from the pivot: arcsin(0.01/0.148) -->
  <joint type="hinge" axis="1 0 0" pos="0 0 0" limited="true"
         range="-0.0676190854 0"
         solreflimit=".01 1" solimplimit=".95 .99 .001"
         stiffness="0.05080" springref="1.0"
         frictionloss="0.01887"
         damping="0.30" armature="2.1422e-3"/>
  <geom group="2" type="box" condim="3" contype="9" conaffinity="0"
        solref=".02 1" solimp=".9 .95 .001" margin="0" priority="1"
        size="0.011313735 0.074 0.0105" pos="0 0.074 0"
        mass="0.120" rgba=".9 .9 .9 1"/>
</default>

<default class="black_key">
  <!-- travel depth 0.008 m at 0.096 m from the pivot: arcsin(0.008/0.096) -->
  <joint type="hinge" axis="1 0 0" pos="0 0 0" limited="true"
         range="-0.08333333333 0"
         solreflimit=".01 1" solimplimit=".95 .99 .001"
         stiffness="0.03295" springref="1.0"
         frictionloss="0.01224"
         damping="0.30" armature="9.0000e-4"/>
  <geom group="2" type="box" condim="3" contype="9" conaffinity="0"
        solref=".02 1" solimp=".9 .95 .001" margin="0" priority="1"
        size="0.00475 0.048 0.01" pos="0 0.048 0"
        mass="0.062" rgba=".1 .1 .1 1"/>
</default>

<!--
  DERIVATION, for the record. Balance weight 35 gf, friction 13 gf.
    white, arm 0.148 m
      stiffness    = 0.035 * 9.80665 * 0.148 / 1.0 rad = 0.05080 N m/rad
      frictionloss = 0.013 * 9.80665 * 0.148           = 0.01887 N m
    black, arm 0.096 m
      stiffness    = 0.035 * 9.80665 * 0.096 / 1.0 rad = 0.03295 N m/rad
      frictionloss = 0.013 * 9.80665 * 0.096           = 0.01224 N m
  springref 1.0 rad is far outside the 0.068 rad of travel, so spring force
  varies by under 7 per cent across the stroke and reads as near constant.
